  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    Sleek☽ Caemomot is a fire-scarred trading post carved into The Berg's western flank, situated three tunnel-hours below The Drowned Ledge, a corruption-blighted Nightshelf territory whose influence seeps visibly through the post's northern wall. The site has passed through at least four distinct governing authorities. Its tunnels retain the architectural signatures of each, layered over one another in a stratigraphy that is more legible than any written record the post possesses.
    
    Locals call it the Seep-Market, though never to the official merchants' faces.
    
    ## Architectural History and Successive Occupations
    
    The main cavern was excavated by the Shadow Covens, a Nightshelf occult order, an estimated three ages ago. The original design — high-ceilinged, efficiently laid out — reflects practitioners who understood load-bearing stone and ventilation in deep tunnels. The Icebound Exchange, Aurora Stack's dominant merchant guild, subsequently carved crude expansion tunnels that intersect the older Shadow Coven geometry; the collapsed trading galleries where these two architectures collide remain partially accessible. The Wake-Singers, an orca-aligned cult active at The Corpse Current, later claimed the space and painted territorial glyphs over Exchange markings. These glyphs are no longer maintained.
    
    The Shadow Covens lost control of the post seasons ago by most accounts, though traders still set offerings at the old Coven altar before conducting business — a habit that persists despite, or perhaps because of, the absence of anyone who could explain its original purpose. A carved flipper-print near the eastern tunnel-mouth has been worn smooth by decades of traffic. Its maker is unidentified.
    
    The Drowned Ledge's corruption now bleeds through the northern wall in bioluminescent weeping. The fungi that bloom in these seeps glow sickly blue and carry the smell of deep-pressure and organic wrongness — a wound in the stone that shows no sign of closing. Their glow pulses with a rhythm unconnected to any identifiable draft or temperature change. The seepage runs along channels that predate the Collective☽ Of Led∴merch, and the corrupted ice-stone substrate holds thermal memory-impressions from fire-workings older than any living practitioner. The braziers still burn on salvaged fire-cores, jealously rationed, though the light they cast behaves incorrectly, pooling in corners geometry cannot account for.
    
    ## Substrate Properties
    
    The bioluminescent fungi in the northern wall seepage respond to fire-core proximity by accelerating growth and brightening — the corrupted substrate interacts with fire-core heat not as a destructive agent but as a thermal exchange medium, metabolizing excess energy through its bioluminescent chemistry. This property has proven exploitable in fire-core conduit design, with housings carved from the corrupted ice-stone itself capable of extending burn cycles beyond the fracture limits of conventional bone-lattice.
    
    ## The Orca Incursion and Structural Collapse
    
    The western shelf collapsed during the Orca Incursion, severing every tunnel between the western flank and the eastern depots and destroying the last fire-core supply route on that side. Orca shore-breakers had already compromised overland routes by hammering the surface ice to failure, forcing all remaining traffic through corruption-infected underground passages.
    
    Tunnel-clearing operations led by Augur ☽'Milucaum~, a Nightshelf tunnel-worker and creator of the volcanic glass blade Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~, were conducted beneath the cavern during this period. The chronicle account *Six∴Falls* describes Augur wielding the blade against threats in the deep passages; the nature of those threats is not specified with the precision one might wish.
    
    ## Subterranean Phenomena
    
    The eastern vein beneath the post was the site of the **Ofundis∴flux** — a catastrophic convergence of aurora-fire and volcanic heat that crystallized the surrounding passages and sealed the tunnels at the convergence point. At least one miner, identified in the chronicle *Keelo's Reaching* as Keelo, was consumed. Rescue crews recovered a shard of anomalous density from the crystallized vein face three days after the event. The shard reportedly produced a faint tone when freed. I note the three-day interval without further comment; readers familiar with my annotations elsewhere will understand why.
    
    Deeper still, the artifact Crypt-seal☽ Teneecloris~ — a crystallized shard of raw power that emerged during the Pta∴extinct — has transformed the floor substrate into translucent crystal overlaying vast void spaces. The crystal pulses with light from below and is structurally unreliable; it can fracture under a traveler's weight. Near the fissures, whisper-zones carry fragments that resemble tactical intelligence — channel numbers, breach coordinates. Some of this information has proven accurate enough to lure the unwary closer, which is precisely the problem.
    
    ## Trade Operations
    
    Access requires a bone-clasp or guild introduction. The post is currently controlled by the Collective☽ Of Led∴merch, a Nightshelf merchant guild that moves graded fire-cores through the galleries.
    
    | Commodity | Details |
    |---|---|
    | Fire-cores (Class II–IV) | Priced in fire-crystal or aurora-shard equivalent; old-vein stock burns purple-spectrum at double the warmth-months of surface-sourced alternatives |
    | Deep-seam relics | Catalogued by resonance-weight, sold as-found, documentation in cipher |
    | Accepted currency | Fire-crystal, aurora-shard equivalent only; surface scrip and Accord-logged tender refused |
    | Authentication | Guild seal in fire-core wax from the Swaleed~ Luminary's unextinguished flame |
    
    The Collective claims its authentication seal has held through three wars. Every transaction passes through the galleries under this mark.
    
    Pactum∴ne, the Nightshelf trade charter governing fire-core shipments, had its artifact-governance clauses sealed with assessors' thumbprints in ash beneath this cavern — a detail the Collective does not advertise but which the substrate remembers clearly enough.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771588003858_0", anchor: "layered over one another in a stratigraphy that is more legible than any written record the post possesses", text: "How extraordinary. A trading post that destroyed, lost, or never bothered to create documentation of its own governance — and yet anyone with tunnel-reading training can walk the galleries and reconstruct the sequence of occupation from chisel-angles and ceiling heights alone. The Shadow Covens built for permanence. The Exchange built for throughput. The Wake-Singers painted over both without understanding either. The Collective moved in and changed nothing structural at all, which is itself a signature. Four administrations, zero surviving charters, and the walls remember everything.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588003858_1", anchor: "traders still set offerings at the old Coven altar before conducting business", text: "Every faction, in every era, believed their rituals were rational responses to understood forces. These traders would be offended by the comparison.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588003858_2", anchor: "the nature of those threats is not specified with the precision one might wish", text: "*Six∴Falls* specifies quite clearly. Augur's blade was forged from volcanic glass — pressure-depth craft, not aurora work. The chronicle names what came through the deep passages using terminology I recognize from pre-penguin impressions in the Foundation Depths. I chose the evasion in the main text because the Nightshelf coded those passages for a reason, and because the Council commissioned a retrospective, not a bestiary. But the margin can hold this: whatever Augur fought, it was not orca.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588003858_3", anchor: "Some of this information has proven accurate enough to lure the unwary closer", text: "Breach coordinates. Channel numbers. Useful intelligence, freely given by a wound in the stone. See my Pta∴extinct annotation: the corruption's most effective vector is not madness — it is usefulness. Sleek☽ Caemomot is the commercial proof.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588003858_4", anchor: "The Collective claims its authentication seal has held through three wars", text: "Which three? The Collective's charter postdates the Faction Wars. If they are counting the Orca Incursion, the Hisper∴vi territorial disputes, and the E'frostling schism, that is a generous definition of 'war' applied selectively to inflate continuity. If they mean something else, they have not shared their periodization with me, and I would very much like to see it.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
